```python
import heapq

def build_huffman_tree(freqs):  # freqs: {symbol: frequency}
    heap = [[f, [sym, ""]] for sym, f in freqs.items()]
    heapq.heapify(heap)
    while len(heap) > 1:
        lo = heapq.heappop(heap)
        hi = heapq.heappop(heap)
        for pair in lo[1:]:
            pair[1] = '0' + pair[1]
        for pair in hi[1:]:
            pair[1] = '1' + pair[1]
        heapq.heappush(heap, [lo[0] + hi[0]] + lo[1:] + hi[1:])
    return sorted(heap[0][1:], key=lambda p: (len(p[-1]), p))
```

Each `heappush`/`heappop` is O(log n), and there are `n - 1` merge rounds, so the whole build is O(n log n) — the standard complexity this technique buys you over any less structured approach to picking "the two smallest" repeatedly.
